    "question_text": "Rather than dumping sewage sludge in the North Sea, it has been suggested that it could be used as fertiliser on agricultural land. However, sewage sludge often contains waste from industry which is contaminated with heavy metals, some of which, e.g. zinc and cadmium, can be absorbed by plants. Zinc is generally present in the sewage in greater amounts than other metals and is a problem for crop production because it will kill plants at concentrations which are too low to cause toxicity to animals. However, concentrations of cadmium can become high enough to make plants dangerous as food without the cadmium having any obvious effect on the growth of plants. Which one of the following can be inferred from claims made in the passage?",
    "answer_options": {
      "A": "If cadmium and zinc were removed from sewage sludge, it could safely be used as fertiliser.",
      "B": "The greater risk to health from sewage sludge used as fertiliser would be its contamination with zinc.",
      "C": "If sewage sludge contains cadmium, it is unwise to use it as a fertiliser.",
      "D": "There is no future in using sewage sludge as a fertiliser.",
      "E": "Dumping sewage sludge in the North Sea is safer than using it as fertiliser."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Official answer: C. This question is tagged as Numerical Reasoning & Problem-Solving; sub-type: Relevant Selection; topic tag: Data Filtering & Table Extraction. Method: Locate the exact row, column or chart value needed for the target question. Use the highlighted wording to identify the task, then use the distractor analysis to understand why plausible wrong options fail.",
