    "question_text": "If someone tries to rescue someone from a burning roof, slips, drops them, and they die, we don’t class them as a murderer. This is because we believe that judgements should be based on the intention behind an action, not the outcome. Whether or not we like to admit it, however, our moral judgements are sometimes shaped by mere luck. Imagine someone gets into a car while drunk and drives home. We all agree that’s bad. Now imagine another person who does the same, but this person kills a pedestrian. We naturally think that this action is worse. But surely the only difference is that the first driver got lucky, while the second driver was unlucky. The second driver did not intend to kill a pedestrian any more than the first did. Which one of the following best expresses the main conclusion of the above argument?",
    "answer_options": {
      "A": "If someone accidentally kills someone while trying to rescue them we don’t consider them a murderer.",
      "B": "We believe that judgements about the morality of an action should be based on the intention behind it.",
      "C": "Despite what we may like to think, our moral judgements can be influenced by luck.",
      "D": "The only difference between the two drivers is that the first driver got lucky.",
      "E": "The second driver did not intend to kill someone any more than the first did."
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Official answer: C. This question is tagged as Critical Thinking; sub-type: Identifying the Main Conclusion; topic tag: Identifying the Main Conclusion. Method: Separate background, reasons and examples from the author's central claim. Use the highlighted wording to identify the task, then use the distractor analysis to understand why plausible wrong options fail.",
